UNITED STATES CONTRIBUTIONS TO HUMANITY

A Historical and Technological Assessment of American Contributions to Global Civilization

Author: Ouadi Maakoul + SI TEAM
Date: 22 October 2026
Status: Final theoretical doctoral dissertation — Version 1.1
Supersedes: v0.1 (narrative draft), V0.2 (data files), v1.0 (first integrated draft)

Research status declaration. This document is a complete theoretical specification, not a completed empirical demonstration. It presents the framework, the formalized instruments, the corrected confidence calculus, the revised source-evaluation grid, the expanded outcome-measurement fields, the pre-registered reproducibility protocol, and the designed pilots. It does not present verified historical findings. No claim is marked verified on the basis of appearing here. The version number is 1.1 because it applies five corrections to v1.0: (i) the confidence-calculus contradiction is resolved by joint-cap application; (ii) claim and source totals are reconciled and the principle of automatic generation from master files is adopted; (iii) the mechanical source hierarchy is replaced by a proposition-specific evaluation grid; (iv) human-outcome measurement is expanded beyond the IE scale; (v) the reproducibility protocol is recast as a pre-registered test designed to give the framework a real chance to fail.

---

Abstract

This dissertation develops a reproducible, claim-level framework for assessing contributions made by the United States to global civilization, and specifies four pilots: semiconductor and integrated-circuit development; the Internet ecosystem; the Global Positioning System; and smallpox eradication. Contribution is treated not as a national property but as a bounded set of evidence-linked roles distributed across a layered historical process — precursor knowledge, conception, demonstration, disclosure, patenting or specification, engineering and manufacturability, institutional and resource provision, standards and policy, production and diffusion, and human outcomes and externalities.

The central instrument is a Contribution Ledger: a version-controlled set of falsifiable claims, a source register recording provenance, exact locators, and evidentiary limits, and a claim–source relation table that assigns each source to a specific proposition and an independence lineage. Five recurring attribution errors motivate the design — category collapse, evidence collapse, attribution collapse, impact collapse, and synthesis collapse — and each is addressed by a structural rule.

The dissertation makes six contributions. First, it formalizes a confidence calculus in which chronology confidence and attribution confidence are independently scored and subjected to a joint cap that resolves the contradiction between the single-lineage rule and the general independence rule (Chapter 8). Second, it replaces a mechanical source hierarchy with a proposition-specific evaluation grid in which direct relevance, archival proximity, institutional interest, locator precision, and independence are evaluated separately and no single dimension is decisive (Chapter 9). Third, it expands human-outcome measurement beyond the descriptive IE scale by adding separate fields for methodological quality, population specification, distributional profile, and causal uncertainty, so that a causal study is not treated as robust merely because it uses a counterfactual (Chapter 11). Fourth, it recasts reproducibility as a pre-registered test with stated failure conditions, not as a rhetorical claim, and specifies how disagreements are reported (Chapter 12). Fifth, it reconciles the claim and source totals and adopts the principle that all manuscript counts must be generated automatically from the master files, never entered manually (Chapter 19). Sixth, it supplies a normative frame — a capability-based conception of human outcome — that prevents availability from being mistaken for benefit (Chapter 4).

The framework produces no composite national score. It ranks nothing. Its objective is a transparent, contestable, revisable record of historically bounded contributions and their demonstrated consequences, including costs, dependencies, distributional inequities, and harms. The governing principle is stated once and applied throughout: no nation receives credit for an outcome merely because its name appears on the cover of the history.

Keywords: history of technology; United States; contribution attribution; science and technology studies; evidence provenance; independent invention; public infrastructure; human outcomes; reproducible historical research; contribution ledger.

