PART II — FRAMEWORK

6. Theoretical Framework: Contribution as a Layered Process

6.1 The eight layers

Contribution is modeled as roles distributed across eight analytical layers. Layers are not a required sequence; a process may loop, split, fail, or be independently rediscovered.

Layer Name Content
A Knowledge and precursors Prior theory, discoveries, instruments, materials, related systems
B Conception and design Documented proposal, architecture, design idea
C Demonstration and disclosure Working demonstration, presentation, paper, technical report
D Engineering and manufacturability Reliability, repeatability, maintainability, economy, producibility
E Institutional and resource contribution Funding, procurement, laboratories, universities, workforce, management
F Standards, law, and policy Protocols, interfaces, regulation, licensing, procurement rules
G Production, diffusion, and access Volume, distribution, deployment, affordability, reach, integration
H Human outcomes and externalities Evidenced benefits, harms, exclusion, dependency, unintended effects

6.2 Formal notation

Let C be a claim. Define C as a tuple

C = ⟨id, π, e, a, t, L, s, d, o⟩

where π is the proposition (a falsifiable sentence), e the event type, a the attributed actor(s), t the temporal specification, L ⊆ {A,…,H} the layer(s) the claim concerns, s the source set, d the dependency set (international and precursor), and o the outcome specification. A claim is well-formed if π is a single falsifiable sentence and |L| ≥ 1. A claim is sourced if s ≠ ∅ and every sᵢ has a locator. A claim is audit-complete only if it meets the criteria of Chapter 25.

6.3 Structural rules (mapping errors to rules)

Error Structural rule
E1 Category collapse §6.2 well-formedness + §21 claim splitting: distinct milestones → distinct claims
E2 Evidence collapse §7.2 source fields + §9 proposition-specific evaluation grid
E3 Attribution collapse §6.2 bounded-actor requirement + §8.6 country-attribution cap
E4 Impact collapse §4 normative frame + §11 expanded outcome fields
E5 Synthesis collapse §10.5 decomposition rule for synthesis claims

6.4 Theoretical proposition

P1. National contribution is best represented as a bounded set of evidence-linked roles across a layered process, not as an exclusive ownership label attached to an outcome. P1 is a methodological hypothesis evaluated by the pilots, not an empirical claim about any nation.

7. Methodology: Ledger, Register, and Relation Table

7.1 The three instruments

Instrument 1 — Claim Ledger. Fields: Claim ID; Version; Pilot; Exact falsifiable claim; Event type; Conception date; Demonstration date; Public disclosure date; Patent filing date; Patent grant date; Commercial production date; Mass adoption date; Chronology confidence; Individuals; Institutions; Funding; Contribution type; Precise US contribution; International dependencies; Primary sources; Independent corroboration; Alternative explanation tested; Global adoption evidence; Human outcomes; Costs and risks; Attribution confidence; Impact evidence (IE); Methodological quality (MQ); Population specification (POP); Distributional profile (DIST); Causal uncertainty (CU); Open questions; Revision log; Research status.

Instrument 2 — Source Register. Fields: Source ID; Title; Author/institution; Publication or event date; Source type; Stable URL/DOI/archive ID; Page/section/locator; Exact quotation; What it establishes; What it does not establish; Source lineage/dependency; Direct relevance to linked proposition(s); Archival proximity; Institutional interest; Locator precision; Access date; Verification status; Notes.

Instrument 3 — Relation Table. Fields: Link ID; Claim ID; Source ID; Relationship (supports / contradicts / qualifies / contextualizes); Specific proposition supported or contradicted; Locator; Independence group; Probative profile (R, P, I, L, D); Confidence contribution; Notes.

7.2 The evidentiary separation principle

A source is evaluated for a specific proposition, not globally. A patent is strong evidence that a filing was made and claims asserted; it is not evidence of priority, successful implementation, commercial success, or benefit. A contemporaneous announcement accurately records what was said; it does not verify the technical claim. A later scholarly study may be stronger on chronology where it critically evaluates notebooks, correspondence, and patents. The register therefore requires "what it establishes" and "what it does not establish" as mandatory, source-specific fields, and the relation table assigns each source to a specific proposition.

7.3 Event-specific chronology

For each technology or program the ledger separates: precursor work; conception; working demonstration; public disclosure; patent filing; patent grant; engineering/manufacturing milestone; commercial production; standardization or policy change; adoption and human outcomes. Missing evidence is recorded as "not established" (the record does not justify a date) — not as "not applicable," which is reserved for fields that genuinely do not apply.

7.4 Relation-table discipline

A list of URLs in a claim row is a database error: it does not specify which sentence each source supports. The relation table makes each source–sentence–lineage assignment explicit and permits one source to bear on multiple claims with different evidentiary roles.

8. The Confidence Calculus

This chapter resolves the contradiction identified in v1.0, where the general independence cap and the single-lineage cap were stated separately and could conflict.

8.1 Dimensions

Three dimensions are scored independently on ordinal scales. They are never summed or averaged into a composite.

Chronology Confidence (CC) ∈ {1, 2, 3, 4}
CC = 4 if a primary contemporaneous record fixes the date and an independent lineage corroborates it.
CC = 3 if a primary contemporaneous record fixes the date or two independent lineages corroborate.
CC = 2 if only a single retrospective synthesis or a single derivative account gives the date.
CC = 1 if the date is undated, disputed, or inferred without a locator. CC = 1 is a marker of indeterminacy, not evidence of weak support for the claim. A claim scored CC = 1 is not thereby "less true"; it is unsupported at the current evidentiary state. The value must never be cited as if it counted in favor of the proposition.

Attribution Confidence (AC) ∈ {1, 2, 3, 4}
AC = 4 if ≥ 2 independent lineages support the attribution and an explicit alternative-explanation test is recorded and passed.
AC = 3 if ≥ 2 independent lineages support the attribution, or 1 independent plus a documented disconfirmation test.
AC = 2 if a single lineage supports it and no independent disconfirmation exists.
AC = 1 if attribution rests on reputation, national label, or unsourced synthesis. AC = 1 is a marker of indeterminacy, not evidence of weak support. The same interpretive rule as for CC applies.

Impact Evidence (IE) ∈ {0, 1, 2, 3, 4}
IE = 0 no outcome claim.
IE = 1 capability exists (artifact, service, or policy in force) — not an outcome.
IE = 2 documented diffusion or access (counts, coverage, adoption) for a defined population.
IE = 3 measured outcome for a defined population (performance, safety, economic, health).
IE = 4 causal outcome study with counterfactual or quasi-experimental identification.

IE is a descriptive indicator of the type of outcome evidence, not of its quality. Quality is recorded separately in the fields of §11.

8.2 The joint cap (resolution of the v1.0 contradiction)

For any claim, let k = number of distinct independence groups among its linked sources. Two caps are defined:

General independence cap (§8.5 in v1.0): C_gen = min(4, 2 + k).
Single-lineage anti-inflation cap (§8.3 in v1.0): C_anti = 1 if k = 0; 2 if k = 1; 4 if k ≥ 2.

The effective cap applied to both CC and AC is the more restrictive of the two:

C_max = min(C_gen, C_anti).

Explicitly:

k C_gen = min(4, 2+k) C_anti C_max = min Interpretation
0 2 1 1 Unsourced; indeterminate, not weak support
1 3 2 2 Single lineage; no claim above retrospective
2 4 4 4 Two independent lineages; full range available
≥2 4 4 4 Full range available

The table is applied mechanically. A claim resting on one lineage (e.g., only CHM Silicon Engine sources) is capped at CC = 2 and AC = 2 even if the source is a professional-society synthesis. A claim with no linked source is capped at CC = 1 and AC = 1, and those values are recorded as indeterminate, not as weak evidence for the proposition. The manuscript's audit dashboard (Chapter 19) must apply C_max, not either cap alone.

8.3 Rules of use

R1. Every populated score requires a one-line rationale in the audit record.
R2. Scores are recorded per claim, not per pilot.
R3. A claim may carry a date at CC = 4 while carrying impact at IE = 1. Mixed profiles are normal and are the point.
R4. "Not established" is a research task; "not applicable" is a field exclusion. Neither is evidence for or against the claim.
R5. A claim scored CC = 1 or AC = 1 must carry the label indeterminate in the audit record, and the score must not be cited in any summary as if it were a weak positive finding.

8.4 Country-attribution cap

Attribution to a bounded actor (individual, team, institution) and attribution to a country are distinct. Country attribution requires an additional step: showing that the actor's work occurred within or was materially enabled by US institutions, funding, or territory, and that no disqualifying international dependency dominates the specific claim. Country attribution is therefore capped one band below actor attribution unless the international-dependency field is populated and tested. Formally:

AC_country ≤ AC_actor − 1 when the dependency field is unpopulated or untested.

8.5 Anti-inflation rule (restated)

No claim may carry CC ≥ 3 or AC ≥ 3 on the strength of a single source lineage. The joint cap of §8.2 is the operative rule; the anti-inflation rule is its k = 1 case.

9. Source Evaluation Grid

This chapter replaces the mechanical hierarchy of v1.0. A contemporary source is not automatically superior to a later historical study, and an original document can establish that a proposition was published at a date without proving that it was realized or caused a result.

9.1 The grid

For each source s and proposition p, probative weight is recorded as a profile, not a scalar:

W(s, p) = ⟨R, P, I, L, D⟩

where:

R — Direct relevance ∈ {0, 1, 2, 3}
R = 0: does not address p.
R = 1: contextual or indirect.
R = 2: directly addresses p but partially.
R = 3: directly and decisively addresses p.

P — Archival proximity ∈ {1, 2, 3}
P = 1: retrospective synthesis without documented archival work.
P = 2: secondary study with documented archival access, or retrospective primary statement.
P = 3: primary contemporaneous record.

I — Institutional interest ∈ {−1, 0, +1}
I = −1: source has an interest adverse to the proposition (e.g., a competitor's record confirming a rival's priority).
I = 0: neutral or no identifiable interest.
I = +1: source has an interest favorable to the proposition (e.g., a company history asserting its own priority).
Institutional interest is recorded as a caution, not a disqualification: an interested source may still be accurate, and an adverse-interest source may be especially probative.

L — Locator precision ∈ {0, 1, 2}
L = 0: no locator.
L = 1: section-level locator.
L = 2: exact page, passage, figure, or record identifier.

D — Independence group (categorical; not ordinal).

Source type is recorded as metadata T but does not determine W. A patent, a museum page, a peer-reviewed article, and a government statement are different kinds of source; the grid asks what each can establish for the specific proposition at issue.

9.2 Adjudication

Conflict between sources is resolved by comparing profiles dimension by dimension, not by a global ranking.

T1. Direct relevance dominates for the proposition at issue. A source with R = 3 outranks a source with R = 1 for that proposition, even if the latter has higher P.
T2. Higher P breaks ties at equal R, unless the higher-P source has I = +1 and the lower-P source has I = 0 or −1, in which case the locator-precision and interest dimensions must be weighed explicitly and the reasoning recorded.
T3. Higher L breaks ties at equal R and P.
T4. Independence breaks ties at equal R, P, and L: a source in a distinct lineage outranks a derivative one.
T5. No dimension is decisive alone. Where the profile comparison does not yield a clear precedence, the conflict is recorded as unresolved and both sources are retained with their profiles. Unresolved conflicts are findings, not failures.

9.3 What counts as "conflict"

Conflict exists when two sources disagree on a proposition, a date, an attribution, or an outcome. Where sources differ in scope rather than contradict, this is recorded as a qualifier, not a conflict.

9.4 Disconfirming-evidence rule

Every claim must record at least one disconfirming test: a question whose answer, if affirmative, would weaken the claim. Absence of evidence is not evidence of absence; incomplete archives are recorded as limitations, not as support.

9.5 Synthesis-claim decomposition (E5 rule)

A synthesis claim (e.g., "Internet commercialization resulted from interacting technical, funding, policy, and market processes") cannot be verified as a whole. It must be decomposed into at least three component causal claims, each independently testable, before any component may be scored above IE = 1. Synthesis claims are flagged in the ledger and paired with a decomposition plan.

10. Adjudication, Disconfirming Evidence, and Synthesis

10.1 The adjudication procedure

When sources conflict, the grid of §9.2 is applied. The relation table records the probative profile of each source for the disputed proposition. The adjudication outcome is one of: resolved in favor of source A (with profile comparison recorded), resolved in favor of source B, or unresolved (both retained).

10.2 Tie-breaking rules

Restated from §9.2 for completeness: direct relevance dominates; archival proximity breaks ties at equal relevance, subject to the interest caution; locator precision breaks ties at equal relevance and proximity; independence breaks ties at equal relevance, proximity, and precision; no dimension is decisive alone.

10.3 What counts as "conflict" and "qualifier"

As §9.3.

10.4 Disconfirming-evidence rule

As §9.4. Every claim records at least one disconfirming test. The test is stated in the ledger's "Alternative explanation tested" field with the result (passed, failed, or untested). A claim with an untested disconfirming test cannot exceed AC = 2.

10.5 Synthesis-claim decomposition

As §9.5. Synthesis claims are flagged and decomposed. The decomposition is recorded in the ledger's "Open questions" field with at least three component claims specified.

11. Human Outcome Measurement

This chapter expands the IE scale of v1.0. A causal study is not robust merely because it uses a counterfactual; methodological quality, population specification, distribution, and causal uncertainty are recorded separately.

11.1 The descriptive indicator (IE)

IE remains as defined in §8.1: a descriptive indicator of the type of outcome evidence, from 0 (no outcome claim) to 4 (causal study). IE is not a quality score.

11.2 The quality and distribution fields

Four additional fields are recorded for every claim with IE ≥ 2:

MQ — Methodological quality ∈ {1, 2, 3, 4}
MQ = 1: anecdotal or undocumented.
MQ = 2: descriptive statistics or case report without identification strategy.
MQ = 3: observational study with stated identification strategy and acknowledged limitations.
MQ = 4: quasi-experimental or experimental design with explicit counterfactual, bias assessment, and external-validity discussion.

POP — Population specification
A statement of the population(s) for which the outcome is evidenced, and the limits of generalization. "Global" is not a population specification; "users of consumer GPS receivers in OECD countries, 2005–2015" is.

DIST — Distributional profile
A statement of documented inequality in access, benefit, or harm across the specified population. Where distribution is unmeasured, the field records "unmeasured" and the claim cannot be scored as a universal benefit.

CU — Causal uncertainty
A statement of the principal threats to causal inference: omitted variables, selection, reverse causation, measurement error, external-validity limits. Where the threats are unassessed, the field records "unassessed."

11.3 Reporting rule

Effective impact strength is reported as the profile ⟨IE, MQ, POP, DIST, CU⟩, never as a single number. A claim with IE = 4 and MQ = 1 is reported as causal design with low methodological quality, not as strong evidence. A claim with IE = 3, MQ = 4, and DIST = "unmeasured" is reported as measured outcome with high methodological quality and unmeasured distribution, and cannot be cited as evidence of universal benefit.

11.4 The anti-universalization rule

A favorable outcome result does not become proof of universal benefit. The DIST field must be populated before any outcome claim is described as a benefit "for humanity." Where DIST is unmeasured, the claim is described as a benefit for the specified population, with the distributional limit stated.

12. Reproducibility Protocol

This chapter recasts the protocol of v1.0 as a pre-registered test designed to give the framework a real chance to fail. The 80% threshold and the one-band tolerance are design choices, not validated thresholds, and are presented as hypotheses to be tested.

12.1 Definition

Definition 12.1 (Reproduction). A claim is reproduced when an independent reviewer, given only the source packet (the claim text, the source register entries for its linked sources, and the relation-table links, without the narrative chapter), can:
(i) locate every cited source;
(ii) verify every quotation against the locator;
(iii) assign CC, AC, IE, and the §11.2 fields independently;
(iv) state at least one alternative explanation;
and reaches scores that differ from the original by at most one band on each ordinal dimension.

12.2 Pre-registered criteria

The following are pre-registered as hypotheses to be tested, not as validated thresholds:

H1. With ≥ 2 independent reviewers, ≥ 80% of reviewer–dimension pairs will fall within one band of the original.
H2. ≥ 1 reviewer will independently raise the same alternative explanation recorded in the ledger.
H3. Disagreements will be concentrated in AC and in the §11.2 fields, not in CC.

These hypotheses may fail. A failure of H1, H2, or H3 is a finding about the framework, not a failure of the reviewers. The protocol is designed so that the framework can be shown to be less reproducible than claimed.

12.3 Procedure

1. Select ≥ 3 claims spanning ≥ 2 pilots, including at least one claim with k = 1 and one with k ≥ 2.
2. Recruit ≥ 2 independent reviewers without access to the narrative.
3. Reviewers complete a blind audit form covering CC, AC, IE, MQ, POP, DIST, CU, and alternative explanations.
4. Compute per-dimension agreement and per-claim agreement.
5. Report disagreements by dimension, not only as a global pass/fail. A claim that passes globally but fails on AC is reported as an AC disagreement.
6. Report non-reproducible cases in full, with the reviewers' reasoning.
7. Record any protocol modifications made after pre-registration, with the reason.

12.4 Reporting and consequences

The reproducibility report states, per claim: pass/fail on each hypothesis, band deviations by dimension, alternative explanations raised, and archival gaps identified. A claim that fails is weakened, and the weakening is logged under revision control. The report is published alongside the framework, including failures.

12.5 Pre-registration requirement

The protocol must be deposited in a public registry before the review is run. The deposit records: the claims selected, the criteria H1–H3, the analysis plan, and the date. This prevents post-hoc adjustment of thresholds to manufacture a pass.

13. Case Selection Rationale

The four pilots are selected on an explicit two-criterion logic:

Criterion A (global reach): the contribution is widely used or widely cited as a US contribution across more than one world region.
Criterion B (attribution dispute): the contribution's attribution, chronology, or impact is contested in historiography, policy, or public narrative.

Pilot A (reach) B (dispute) Why selected
Semiconductor/IC Global High (Kilby/Noyce, planar process) Tests category and attribution collapse
Internet Global High (Baran/Davies, ARPANET≠Internet, Web origin) Tests independent invention and synthesis
GPS Global High (precursors, SA, dependency) Tests capability vs. accessibility vs. outcome
Smallpox Global High (distributed agency, WHO vs. national) Stress-tests distributed agency (non-US-origin outcome)

The sample is purposive, not representative. Conclusions do not generalize beyond the framework's demonstrated scope.

