PART III — PILOTS

14. Pilot I: Transistor and Integrated-Circuit Lineage

The semiconductor pilot tests whether the framework distinguishes a sequence of related but non-identical contributions.

Claims. The point-contact transistor demonstrated by Bardeen and Brattain at Bell Laboratories in December 1947 and described in a 1948 publication (US-EL-001) is a distinct event from the 1951 junction-transistor paper of Shockley, Sparks, and Teal (US-EL-002), which is a distinct architecture and stage. The integrated-circuit history is split: Kilby's 1958 solid-circuit demonstration (US-EL-005a) and Noyce's 1959 monolithic patent filing (US-EL-005b) are complementary, not interchangeable. Manufacturability is represented by Hoerni's planar process (US-EL-003) and Lehovec's isolation approach (US-EL-004), which are substantive contributions to Layer D, not footnotes to a singular inventor story.

Initial proposition (P-I). The emergence of practical semiconductor technology is better represented as a network of differentiated contributions than as a single invention attributed to one person or nation.

Current evidentiary state. All five original claims are scaffolded. The only linked source lineage is CHM Silicon Engine (LINEAGE-CHM-SILICONENGINE). Under the joint cap of §8.2, k = 1 gives C_max = 2, so all are capped at CC = 2 and AC = 2. Original papers and patents (Bardeen & Brattain 1948; Shockley et al. 1951; US 3,025,589; US 3,029,366; US 2,981,877) are in the source map but not yet in the register — a traceability gap addressed in Chapter 19.

Open questions. Which dates denote conception vs. demonstration; how patent claims align with working devices; precursor genealogy; alternative manufacturing paths; adoption speed across firms and countries; global diffusion and human outcomes (currently IE = 1 at best).

15. Pilot II: The Internet Ecosystem

The Internet pilot tests category boundaries and independent development.

Claims. Packet switching is associated with Baran at RAND and Davies at the UK NPL; the evidence preserves their independent lines (US-NET-001). ARPANET (US-NET-002) is a funded deployment and research-infrastructure milestone, not the Internet. Cerf and Kahn's 1974 paper (US-NET-003) is a protocol proposal; it does not establish deployment or sole authorship. NSFNET (US-NET-004) requires dated backbone and international records. The Web (US-NET-006) is explicitly non-US origin (CERN), with US networking infrastructure as enabling context. The commercialization synthesis (US-NET-005) is a meta-claim subject to the decomposition rule of §10.5.

Initial proposition (P-II). The Internet's emergence and diffusion cannot be explained by one invention or institution; they require a documented account of interacting technical, institutional, funding, standards, policy, and market processes. This is a causal synthesis and is not verified merely because it sounds balanced.

Current evidentiary state. US-NET-001 and US-NET-003 have single linked sources (LINEAGE-NPL-HISTORY, LINEAGE-ORIGINAL-PAPER-CERF-KAHN); under §8.2, k = 1 gives C_max = 2, so both are capped at CC = 2 and AC = 2. US-NET-002, -004, -005, -006 have no linked sources (k = 0), so the joint cap sets CC = 1 and AC = 1, recorded as indeterminate under §8.3 R5.

Open questions. Relative timing and influence of packet-switching lines; research-network → internetworking transition; international standards and non-US networks; NSFNET's precise scope; commercialization and Web effects on access.

16. Pilot III: Global Positioning System

GPS tests the distinction between creating a technical capability and delivering civilian accessibility.

Claims and splitting. The precursor bundle (US-GPS-001) is split under §21: Transit (US-GPS-001a), TIMATION (US-GPS-001b), Project 621B (US-GPS-001c), and formal program integration in 1973 (US-GPS-001d, formerly US-GPS-003). Precision timing (US-GPS-002) is an enabling element, not the invention of atomic clocks. The Selective Availability discontinuation (US-GPS-004) is the framework's clearest policy-mediation case. Global adoption (US-GPS-005) and dependency/risk (US-GPS-006) are separately scoped.

Initial proposition (P-III). The civilian value of a navigation system depends not only on engineering capability but on policy, access, receiver technology, complementary infrastructure, and resilience.

Current evidentiary state. US-GPS-004 is the strongest claim in the dataset. Official GPS.gov and White House records fix the date, but independence is limited (same policy event, duplicate archival lineage, LINEAGE-US-GPS-POLICY), so under §8.2, k = 1 gives C_max = 2, and CC = 2, AC = 2. The earlier v1.0 assignment of CC = 3 was an error under the joint cap and is corrected here. IE = 2 (announcement-level access); measured accuracy (IE = 3) pending, with MQ, POP, DIST, CU recorded as unassessed. US-GPS-001a–d, -002, -005, -006 have no linked sources and are capped at CC = 1, AC = 1, recorded as indeterminate.

Open questions. Precursor chronologies; clock-type by satellite generation; international metrology dependence; quantified pre/post-SA accuracy with MQ and DIST fields populated; sector and country adoption; incident evidence distinguishing jamming from spoofing.

17. Pilot IV: Smallpox Eradication

Smallpox is the stress test because the outcome cannot plausibly be owned by one country or one scientist. It tests whether the ledger preserves distributed agency and differentiated credit while still identifying the precise contributions of US institutions and personnel.

Minimal claim set. Six claims instantiate the pilot:

· US-SP-001 — The Soviet Union proposed a WHO smallpox eradication program in 1958 (non-US origin; anchor claim).
· US-SP-002 — WHO's 1966 intensified program adopted surveillance-containment alongside mass vaccination.
· US-SP-003 — US CDC field personnel and D. A. Henderson (program lead from 1966) contributed to WHO coordination and field operations.
· US-SP-004 — US vaccine production and supply contributed to the global vaccine stock.
· US-SP-005 — National programs and local health teams performed vaccination, surveillance, and case containment.
· US-SP-006 — Smallpox was certified eradicated in 1980 (outcome claim; IE only after decomposition under §10.5).

Initial proposition (P-IV). The framework can represent a multinational outcome with differentiated, bounded contributions and without converting a global achievement into a US-centered narrative.

Current evidentiary state. Only SRC-SMALLPOX-001 (Henderson interview) exists; it supports the 1958 Soviet proposal and the 1966 surveillance-containment framing, but not national shares, vaccine quantities, or local-team roles. All six claims are scaffolded. US-SP-001 and -002 have k = 1, capped at CC = 2, AC = 2. US-SP-003, -004, -005, -006 have k = 0, capped at CC = 1, AC = 1, recorded as indeterminate. This pilot is designed and specified but not instantiated; it is the principal empirical task of Phase 6 (Chapter 26).

Open questions. Relative causal weight of vaccination vs. surveillance-containment; US vs. other national contributions; local knowledge and logistics; limits of retrospective attribution.

18. Cross-Pilot Synthesis

Three distinctions recur and constitute the framework's demonstrated payoff:

S1. Existence ≠ manufacturability. The semiconductor pilot separates early devices from the processes that made reliable production possible (Layer C vs. Layer D).

S2. Network milestone ≠ Internet. Packet switching, ARPANET, TCP/IP, NSFNET, the Web, commercialization, and global adoption are related but non-identical objects of explanation (Layers C–G).

S3. Capability ≠ accessibility ≠ outcome. GPS shows that policy can materially alter civilian value of a deployed system (Layers F–H), and that availability is not benefit.

A fourth, meta-level finding: across all pilots, international precursors and parallel development are causal, not decorative. The ledger's dependency field and the country-attribution cap (§8.4) force this into the record rather than leaving it as a caveat.

The synthesis conclusion is provisional: the method appears to expose category errors and to make claims auditable, but its performance must be judged by the pre-registered reproducibility protocol (Chapter 12), which has not yet been run.

