PART V — EVALUATION AND PLAN

22. Worked Audit Exemplars

22.1 Worked source packet — SRC-GPS-001 (GPS.gov, "Selective Availability")

The source is registered with its full field set, including the probative profile of §9.1. For the proposition "SA was discontinued in May 2000," the profile is R = 3 (direct), P = 3 (primary contemporaneous official record), I = +1 (government source with an interest in presenting its own policy favorably), L = 2 (exact section locator pending; currently section-level), D = LINEAGE-US-GPS-POLICY. For the proposition "SA discontinuation improved civilian accuracy by a measured amount," the same source has R = 1 (contextual) and cannot carry the impact claim; that requires independent technical study (IE = 3, MQ ≥ 3).

This exemplar demonstrates the grid working: one source can be strong for a policy-date proposition and weak for an impact proposition, and the profile records both.

22.2 Worked claim audit record — US-GPS-004

Claim. The US government discontinued Selective Availability on 1 May 2000, improving civilian GPS accuracy relative to the prior degraded-signal policy.

Event type and layer. Policy change / service accessibility (Layer F → H).

Date and sources. 2000-05-01; SRC-GPS-001, SRC-GPS-002 (same policy lineage).

Chronology confidence. CC = 2. The date is fixed by official records, but the independence cap of §8.2 (k = 1) gives C_max = 2. The v1.0 assignment of CC = 3 was incorrect under the joint cap and is corrected here.

Attribution confidence. AC = 2, for the same reason. The country-attribution cap of §8.4 does not further reduce AC because the actor is the US government itself.

Impact evidence profile. IE = 2 (diffusion/access documented at announcement level). MQ = 2 (official announcement; no independent identification strategy). POP = "civilian GPS users globally" — to be specified. DIST = unmeasured. CU = unassessed. Under §11.3, the claim is reported as announcement-level access improvement with unmeasured distribution and unassessed causal uncertainty, and cannot be cited as evidence of universal benefit under §11.4.

Alternative explanations tested. Benefits may derive from receiver improvements and differential correction, not SA removal alone; market and policy factors. The test is recorded as untested in the current state, which under §10.4 caps AC at 2.

Disconfirming test. "Is there evidence that measured accuracy did not improve after SA discontinuation?" If affirmative, the claim weakens. The test is recorded as untested.

Revision log. v0.2 → v1.0: CC/AC reduced from "High" to 3. v1.0 → v1.1: CC/AC reduced to 2 under the joint cap of §8.2; impact profile expanded to ⟨IE=2, MQ=2, POP=pending, DIST=unmeasured, CU=unassessed⟩.

Research status. Strongest claim in the dataset; impact attribution open.

This exemplar demonstrates the corrected calculus working: a well-dated, official policy event scores CC = 2, not 3 or 4, because the sources share one policy lineage, and the impact claim cannot exceed IE = 2 until independent measurement populates the MQ, POP, DIST, and CU fields.

23. Limitations

23.1 Theoretical, not empirical. This is a complete theoretical specification. No pilot is fully audited; source packets are incomplete; no reproducibility review has been run. The framework's design is complete; its demonstration is partial by construction.

23.2 Purposive sampling. The four pilots are chosen for reach and dispute, not representativeness. Conclusions do not generalize to all US contributions.

23.3 Pending quotations. Several exemplar quotations remain placeholders pending archival capture. This is disclosed rather than disguised, consistent with the disconfirming-evidence rule and the completion criteria.

23.4 Interpretive residue. Records can be missing, strategically produced, or unevenly preserved. The ledger improves transparency but cannot eliminate uncertainty.

23.5 AI-assisted risk. AI drafting can produce confident synthesis unsupported by documents. Mitigation: source-level verification requirement; no AI-consensus-as-corroboration; human accountability.

23.6 Normative contestability. The capability frame of Chapter 4 is defensible but not the only possible standard; alternative normative frames would change outcome scoring, not the ledger structure.

23.7 Unvalidated thresholds. The 80% and one-band criteria of Chapter 12 are pre-registered hypotheses, not validated thresholds. They may fail. If they fail, the framework is weakened, and the weakening is reported.

24. Ethics and Research Integrity

The study avoids nationalist, anti-nationalist, and presentist distortion. It credits individuals without erasing teams, institutions, public investment, international dependencies, or local users. It reports harms and unequal access alongside benefits, without assuming either cancels the other.

No universal contribution score is produced. A single number would conceal the distinction between discovery, engineering, institutional support, access, and outcome. Where comparisons occur, dimensions and evidence remain visible.

AI systems may assist with search, extraction, drafting, translation, and adversarial critique. Every consequential factual assertion must be traceable to a source a human can inspect. AI-generated consensus is never described as independent corroboration, and no claim is labeled "verified" because it appears in multiple generated drafts. The human research lead retains final interpretive responsibility.

Dual-use and risk disclosure: the GPS dependency claim and any infrastructure-risk findings are reported in a way that does not expose operational vulnerabilities beyond what is already public in technical literature and government advisories.

25. Audit and Completion Criteria

A claim is not complete because its cells contain text. It is audit-complete only when all of the following hold:

AC-1 The claim is one falsifiable sentence with a bounded actor and scope.
AC-2 Distinct milestones are separated (no E1 collapse).
AC-3 Every populated date has a source with a locator; unpopulated fields read "not established" or "not applicable."
AC-4 Every source records "what it establishes" and "what it does not establish."
AC-5 Every source carries a probative profile (§9.1); adjudication outcomes are recorded.
AC-6 Independence groups are assigned; k is computed; the joint cap of §8.2 is applied and recorded.
AC-7 CC, AC, IE, and the §11.2 fields are scored with rationale; CC = 1 and AC = 1 are labeled indeterminate.
AC-8 At least one disconfirming test is recorded and its result noted (§10.4).
AC-9 International dependencies are populated or marked "not established"; the country-attribution cap of §8.4 is applied.
AC-10 Synthesis claims are decomposed or flagged (§10.5).
AC-11 Capability, diffusion, access, outcome, and risk are distinguished; DIST is populated before any outcome is described as a universal benefit (§11.4).
AC-12 An independent reviewer can reproduce the claim from the source packet within one band, or the failure is reported (§12).
AC-13 Revision log records every change, including corrections of prior scores.

A "pass" means the procedure is reproducible at the stated scope, not that every historical controversy is settled. Missing evidence remains visible as a research task.

26. Audit Sequence and Work Plan

The review of v1.0 recommended a sequenced audit before any further narrative revision. That recommendation is adopted as the work plan of v1.1. The phases are ordered so that formal and data defects are corrected before empirical claims are strengthened, and empirical claims are tested before the manuscript is rewritten.

Phase 1 — Formal consistency audit. Verify all equations, rules, cross-references, identifiers, and generated totals. Confirm that every score in the ledger is consistent with the joint cap of §8.2. Confirm that no claim scored CC = 1 or AC = 1 is cited as positive evidence. Output: a consistency report and a corrected ledger.

Phase 2 — Dataset audit. Compare every row of the claim ledger, source register, and relation table. Confirm that every claim has at least one link or is marked "evidence gap"; that every source has a probative profile; that every lineage is assigned; and that the counts of §19.1 are reproduced by script from the master files. Output: a reconciliation report and a script that regenerates the counts.

Phase 3 — Empirical audit of one pilot. Begin with the transistor and integrated circuit, because the claims are bounded and the primary sources (papers, patents) are accessible. Link each proposition to precise documents and locatable passages. Populate the probative profiles. Output: a completed source packet and audit record for US-EL-001, -002, -003, -004, -005a, -005b.

Phase 4 — Contradictory test. Actively seek evidence that could invalidate or reduce the proposed US attribution: earlier precursors, parallel work, non-US contributions, alternative explanations for adoption. Record disconfirming findings in the ledger, not in a footnote. Output: a disconfirmation report per claim.

Phase 5 — Manuscript revision. Only after Phases 1–4, publish a v1.2 consistent with the audited data. The revision preserves the version history and records rejected or weakened claims.

Phase 6 — Smallpox instantiation. Add WHO resolutions, Fenner et al., CDC field records, and national records. Populate the six smallpox claims. Output: a completed smallpox pilot.

Phase 7 — Pre-registered reproducibility review. Deposit the protocol of Chapter 12, run the blind audit of ≥ 3 claims by ≥ 2 reviewers, report by dimension, and publish failures.

