PART VI — CONCLUSION

28. Conclusion

This dissertation begins from a modest but demanding premise: a claim about a country's contribution to humanity should be no stronger than the evidence that supports its specific wording. National labels are useful descriptors but poor substitutes for historical explanation.

The proposed framework treats contribution as a layered, nonlinear, often international process. It separates chronology, attribution, source quality and lineage, adoption, and consequences. It makes alternative explanations mandatory; it formalizes confidence so that "medium" is a computed band rather than a mood, and it resolves the internal contradiction between single-lineage and multi-lineage caps by applying the more restrictive of the two; it replaces a mechanical source hierarchy with a proposition-specific grid in which no single dimension is decisive; it expands outcome measurement beyond the descriptive IE scale so that a causal study is not treated as robust merely because it uses a counterfactual; it recasts reproducibility as a pre-registered test designed to fail; and it requires that all manuscript counts be generated from the master files rather than typed by hand.

The reconciled instantiation demonstrates the framework's discipline. Sixteen sources resolve to ten independence lineages, and several claims are mechanically capped because they rest on a single lineage. The dataset's strongest claim, GPS Selective Availability, scores CC = 2 and AC = 2 under the corrected joint cap — not 3 or 4 — because its official sources document one policy event. That result is the framework working as intended, and it corrects an error in the previous draft.

The semiconductor, Internet, and GPS pilots expose category errors that survive casual synthesis: a first demonstration is not an industry; ARPANET is not the Internet; a navigation capability is not equal civilian benefit. The smallpox pilot is designed to test whether the same ledger can preserve distributed agency without converting a global achievement into a national narrative.

These findings remain provisional until the sequenced audit of Chapter 26 is complete. The purpose is not to award a medal to a nation but to build a record others can inspect, correct, extend, and use. If the framework succeeds, it will make it easier to recognize real contributions without inventing exclusive ownership, and to document human benefits without hiding the costs, dependencies, distributional inequities, and people who made them possible. The governing principle, stated once and applied throughout, is this: no nation receives credit for an outcome merely because its name appears on the cover of the history.

