Figure Q.8 — Proposition-Specific Evaluation Grid (Ch. 9)

```
+-------------------------------------------------------------------+
|        PROPOSITION-SPECIFIC EVALUATION GRID (Ch. 9)               |
+-------------------------------------------------------------------+

   W(s, p) = < R, P, I, L, D >

   R  Direct relevance to proposition p     {0,1,2,3}
      0 = does not address
      1 = contextual / indirect
      2 = directly addresses, partially
      3 = directly and decisively addresses

   P  Archival proximity                    {1,2,3}
      1 = retrospective synthesis, no documented archive
      2 = secondary with archive access OR retrospective primary
      3 = primary contemporaneous record

   I  Institutional interest                {-1,0,+1}
      -1 = adverse to proposition
       0 = neutral
      +1 = favorable to proposition (caution, not disqualifier)

   L  Locator precision                     {0,1,2}
      0 = none
      1 = section-level
      2 = exact page / passage / record

   D  Independence group (categorical, not ordinal)

   ADJUDICATION PRINCIPLES
   -----------------------
   T1  Direct relevance dominates for p
   T2  Higher P breaks ties at equal R
         (unless interest mismatch -> explicit weighing)
   T3  Higher L breaks ties at equal R, P
   T4  Distinct lineage breaks ties at equal R, P, L
   T5  No single dimension decisive
       If unclear -> UNRESOLVED, both sources retained

   SOURCE TYPE IS METADATA, NOT DECISIVE
   -------------------------------------
   A patent is strong for "a filing was made"
   and weak for "the device was built or caused X".
   A contemporary statement is strong for "X was announced"
   and weak for "X was measured".
